adjudication.rationale:
In the newly added `GroupPermissionsV2`, the global management check is:
```java
public boolean canManage() {
  if (root.hasOneAdminRole(AdminRoles.MANAGE_USERS)) return true;
  return hasPermission(null, AdminPermissionsSchema.VIEW, AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
}
```
This means an admin who only has an *all-groups* permission with the `VIEW` scope will satisfy `canManage()` and therefore `requireManage()` (which is typically used to gate mutating group endpoints). In contrast, the per-group overload `canManage(GroupModel group)` correctly requires `MANAGE` only:
```java
return hasPermission(group.getId(), AdminPermissionsSchema.MANAGE);
```
So the global and per-group paths disagree, and the global path is permissive in a way that can enable unintended group modifications when `canManage()` is used to authorize create/update/delete operations.
